  "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+\u2026+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x+7y)}^{10},$ we have $a=x,$ $b=7y,$ and $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+7y)}^{10}=(\\frac{10}{0}){x}^{10}(7y)^{0}+(\\frac{10}{1}){x}^{10-1}(7y)^{1}+(\\frac{10}{2}){x}^{10-2}(7y)^{2}+\u22ef+(\\frac{10}{10}){x}^{10-10}(7y)^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{7}{y}^{3}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{10}{3}){x}^{10-3}(7y)^{3}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{10}{3}){x}^{10-3}(7y)^{3} & =120{x}^{7}\\cdot {7}^{3}{y}^{3} \\\\  & =120{x}^{7}\\cdot 343{y}^{3} \\\\  & =41\\,160{x}^{7}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{7}{y}^{3}$ is $41\\,160.$",
